<p>
  <b>mhs</b> gives the mollified Mindlin half-space Green's functions for
  triangular dislocations, as <i>matrices</i>. Following Cortez, the point force
  is replaced by a smooth blob of width &epsilon; rather than softened by hand,
  so a fault has a finite width and stress is defined on the fault itself, where
  classical dislocation theory returns a value ordinary integration does not
  define. The free surface is carried by the Mindlin image terms, not by a
  meshed boundary.
</p>
<p>
  Because slip is prescribed and the half space is homogeneous, <i>nothing is
  solved</i> &mdash; no linear system, no collocation on a boundary, no box
  truncation. What the library builds is the on-fault interaction matrix that
  Coulomb, rate-and-state and earthquake-cycle models want.
</p>
